<main>

    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Install</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">Get the hub, run <code>parachute init</code>, finish in the setup wizard. One flow &mdash; laptop, server, or hosted deploy.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>The one flow: get the hub, then <code>parachute init</code></h2>

        <p>Every Parachute install converges on the same two moves &mdash; <strong>get a hub</strong>, then <strong>run the setup wizard</strong>. The wizard creates your admin account, sets up your first vault (or imports one, or skips it), and asks how the hub is reached. It runs <strong>in your browser by default</strong> (the <code>/admin/setup</code> page) and is the canonical setup experience on a laptop, an EC2 box, or a Render / Fly deploy. The only thing that differs across environments is how you get the hub running in the first place.</p>

        <h3>On your laptop (or any Mac / Linux box you control)</h3>

        <p>Requires <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank">Bun</a> 1.3.0 or later. Check with <code>bun --version</code>; upgrade with <code>bun upgrade</code>. Then:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>
        </div>

        <aside class="upgrade-callout">
            <strong>Already installed during the beta via the old <code>@openparachute/cli</code> package?</strong>
            <p>Run the rename first so the lead command doesn't conflict with the legacy install:</p>
            <div class="code-inline">
                <code>bun remove -g @openparachute/cli &amp;&amp; bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute upgrade vault</code>
            </div>
            <p>The package was renamed in April 2026; vault's schema and filesystem migrations run automatically on first post-upgrade boot. If you're coming from Vault 0.2.4 (the launch version) and want the full picture, read the <a href="/preview/vault-0.4.5-arc/">0.2.4 &rarr; 0.4.5 upgrade arc</a>. Once you're on hub, <code>parachute init</code> is the front door from here on.</p>
        </aside>

        <p><strong>What <code>parachute init</code> does</strong>, end to end (it's idempotent &mdash; safe to re-run):</p>

        <ol>
            <li><strong>Starts the hub</strong> if it isn't already running (the <code>parachute</code> dispatcher on port <code>1939</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Offers to expose it</strong> so you can reach the wizard from other devices. In a terminal you pick: stay loopback-only (the laptop default), Tailscale Funnel, or Cloudflare Tunnel. On a laptop this defaults to "no thanks &mdash; loopback"; on an SSH'd server it pre-selects Cloudflare. Skip the question with <code>--no-expose-prompt</code>, or pin it non-interactively with <code>--expose none|tailnet|cloudflare</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Installs the vault module</strong> &mdash; always. The module lands so the wizard can offer create / import / skip, but no vault instance is created yet; that's the wizard's call.</li>
            <li><strong>Drops you into the setup wizard.</strong> Browser by default (opens <code>/admin/setup</code>); pick the terminal walk-through instead with <code>--cli-wizard</code>, or force the browser with <code>--browser-wizard</code>. It prints the canonical admin URL either way &mdash; loopback when you're not exposed, the tailnet / Cloudflare FQDN when you are.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>In the wizard</strong> (same three steps in browser and CLI):</p>

        <ol>
            <li><strong>Account.</strong> Create the admin operator for this hub &mdash; username + password. No email, no signup.</li>
            <li><strong>Vault.</strong> <em>Create</em> a fresh vault (default name <code>default</code>), <em>import</em> one from a git repo (a previously-exported Parachute vault on GitHub / GitLab / any HTTPS or SSH remote &mdash; PAT optional for private repos), or <em>skip</em> and create one later from the admin UI. The vault module is installed regardless of which you pick.</li>
            <li><strong>Expose.</strong> Record how the hub is reached (localhost / tailnet / public) so the done screen surfaces the right URLs.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>The done screen hands you a copy-pasteable <code>claude mcp add</code> command (with a freshly-minted operator token), links to start using your vault, and the admin UI for everything else. Verify the stack any time with:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute status</code>
        </div>
        <p>You should see <code>parachute-hub</code> on port <code>1939</code> and <code>parachute-vault</code> on port <code>1940</code>, both <code>running</code>.</p>

        <h3>On a server (EC2, DigitalOcean, Hetzner, any VPS)</h3>

        <p>Identical to the laptop flow &mdash; <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>. The one difference is the exposure step: because you'll want to reach the wizard from your own machine, <code>init</code> detects the SSH session and pre-selects <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel</strong> (your own domain, public HTTPS). Once exposure is up, <code>init</code> prints the reachable <code>/admin/setup</code> URL &mdash; open it in your browser and finish the same wizard.</p>

        <h3>On a hosted deploy (Render / Fly)</h3>

        <p>You don't run <code>parachute init</code> here &mdash; the platform gives you a running, already-exposed hub at a public URL. You land directly in the same setup wizard at <code>&lt;your-hub&gt;/admin/setup</code>, claim admin with a one-time bootstrap token from the deploy logs, and walk Account &rarr; Vault &rarr; Done &mdash; the wizard's <em>Expose</em> step auto-skips on a hosted deploy because the platform already owns the public URL. (On a laptop or VPS the Expose step is part of the walk; on Render / Fly it isn't.) Step-by-step: <a href="/deploy/render/">Deploy to Render &rarr;</a> &middot; <a href="/deploy/fly/">Deploy to Fly &rarr;</a></p>

        <aside class="upgrade-callout">
            <strong>Advanced: install modules one at a time.</strong>
            <p>If you'd rather drive the install by hand instead of through the wizard, <code>parachute install &lt;svc&gt;</code> installs and registers a single module &mdash; e.g. <code>parachute install vault</code> installs Vault, runs its init, creates your first vault, and starts it as a background daemon on <code>127.0.0.1:1940</code>. <code>parachute setup</code> is the older interactive multi-service walk-through (survey what's installed, multi-pick from vault, notes, scribe, threads the prompts up front). Both still work and are additive; <code>parachute init</code> &rarr; wizard is the recommended path.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>2. Use it with your local MCP client</h2>

        <p><strong>Why this step.</strong> The Vault is running but nothing's talking to it yet. MCP is the standard protocol AI clients use to read and write structured memory.</p>

        <p><strong>Claude Code</strong> is the zero-config path &mdash; the install already added your Vault as an MCP server to <code>~/.claude.json</code>, and Claude Code picks it up on the next session. Open a project and try:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><em>"Save a note about today's decision — tag it #product, link to #vault."</em></li>
            <li><em>"What have I written about auth? Pull the notes tagged #auth and summarize."</em></li>
            <li><em>"Show me everything connected to last week's design review."</em></li>
        </ul>

        <p>All nine MCP tools are available: <code>create-note</code>, <code>query-notes</code>, <code>update-note</code>, <code>delete-note</code>, <code>list-tags</code>, <code>update-tag</code>, <code>delete-tag</code>, <code>find-path</code>, <code>vault-info</code>. Memory carries across every session forever; context accretes instead of starting over.</p>

        <p><strong>What you should see.</strong> Run <code>/mcp</code> inside Claude Code &mdash; <code>parachute-vault</code> should appear in the list with the nine tools resolved. If it's missing, re-run the integration:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute-vault mcp-install</code>
        </div>

        <p>The bare command walks you through it interactively. Advanced flags for power users:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>--install-scope local|user|project</code> &mdash; <code>local</code> (default) writes under <code>projects[&lt;cwd&gt;].mcpServers</code> in <code>~/.claude.json</code>, scoping the Vault to one directory. <code>user</code> writes the top-level <code>mcpServers</code>, making the Vault available everywhere. <code>project</code> writes <code>./.mcp.json</code> for checking into a repo.</li>
            <li><code>--scope vault:read|vault:write|vault:admin</code> &mdash; mints a token narrowed to that scope. Useful for read-only clients (search agents, observability tools) that shouldn't have write authority. Default is <code>vault:write</code> (read + write, but not admin).</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Other local MCP clients</strong> &mdash; <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex" target="_blank">Codex</a>, <a href="https://github.com/block/goose" target="_blank">Goose</a>, <a href="https://opencode.ai" target="_blank">OpenCode</a>, <a href="https://cursor.com" target="_blank">Cursor</a>, <a href="https://zed.dev" target="_blank">Zed</a>, <a href="https://github.com/cline/cline" target="_blank">Cline</a>, your own agent &mdash; connect to the same endpoint:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>http://127.0.0.1:1940/vault/default/mcp</code>
        </div>
        <p>Each client has its own way to register an MCP server &mdash; usually a settings entry pointing at that URL plus the <code>pvt_...</code> token from your install. Parachute is client-agnostic; Claude Code just gets the one-line auto-wire.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>3. Want a UI for your notes? (optional)</h2>

        <p><strong>Why this step.</strong> The Vault is a SQLite file behind an API. If you want to <em>see</em> your notes &mdash; browse, search, edit, watch the graph form &mdash; you need a UI. <strong>Parachute Surface</strong> is the UI host module; Notes ships as its first canonical surface.</p>

        <p>Install Surface &mdash; it auto-bootstraps <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface/tree/main/packages/notes-ui" target="_blank">Notes</a> as the first hosted surface:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute install surface</code>
        </div>

        <p><strong>What you should see.</strong> App runs as a background daemon under hub's reverse proxy. Open <a href="http://localhost:1939/surface/notes/" target="_blank"><code>http://localhost:1939/surface/notes/</code></a> in your browser (the legacy <code>http://localhost:1939/notes/</code> URL still works via hub's redirect, for back-compat). Click the vault popover in the header and hit <strong>Connect</strong> next to your vault under "Available from your hub" &mdash; this kicks an OAuth flow through the hub, no token-pasting required. You get note browsing, search, tag views, a graph visualization, voice capture with auto-transcription, and a markdown editor with live preview.</p>

        <p>Notes ships <strong>multi-vault</strong>: one install, every vault on your hub. The popover splits "Connected" (vaults you've OAuthed into) from "Available from your hub" (vaults published at your hub's well-known doc you haven't connected yet). Captures land under hierarchical <code>capture/text</code> / <code>capture/voice</code> tags; Settings &rarr; Larger / Largest scales the whole app for reading; <code>Cmd-=</code>, <code>Cmd--</code>, <code>Cmd-0</code> work the way you'd expect.</p>

        <p><strong>More apps under the same host.</strong> Once App is installed, drop additional custom SPAs in via <code>parachute-surface add &lt;path-to-dist&gt; --name &lt;name&gt; --path &lt;mount-path&gt;</code> (or from the admin SPA). Each lands at <code>&lt;hub&gt;/surface/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, registers as its own OAuth client of the hub, and reads your vault under the scopes it declared. Gitcoin Brain, Unforced Brain, and anything you write next live under the same supervisor.</p>

        <p>Notes is also a starting point: the source is <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface/tree/main/packages/notes-ui" target="_blank">AGPL</a>, Vite + React + TypeScript, talks to the vault over the documented HTTP API. Fork it and build the UI that matches how <em>your</em> brain works &mdash; then host it under App alongside Notes.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>4. Export your vault as portable markdown (optional)</h2>

        <p><strong>Why this step.</strong> Your Vault is a SQLite file &mdash; great for queries, harder to inspect with grep, diff, or your favorite editor. The portable-markdown export gives you a git-trackable projection of the same graph without leaving SQLite as the source of truth.</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute-vault export ~/my-vault-projection</code>
        </div>

        <p><strong>What you should see.</strong> One file per note (named by its path), with frontmatter carrying ID, path, tags, metadata, typed links, and attachments. Tag schemas land in <code>.parachute/schemas/&lt;tag&gt;.yaml</code>, attachment binaries in <code>.parachute/attachments/</code>. Re-export an unchanged vault and the bytes are identical &mdash; clean git diffs.</p>

        <p>Going the other way works too:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute-vault import ~/my-vault-projection --blow-away --yes</code>
        </div>

        <p>That's disaster recovery, vault migration, or "let me edit in Obsidian and re-import the changes" &mdash; same primitive, three uses. Read the <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-patterns/blob/main/cookbook/vault-portable-export.md" target="_blank">portable-export cookbook</a> for format spec, round-trip guarantees, and a nightly git-projection recipe.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What comes next</h2>

        <p>Tailnet exposure (<code>tailscale serve</code> &mdash; private to your own Tailscale devices) is the supported shape today. <strong>Public</strong> HTTPS exposure (Tailscale Funnel or a Cloudflare Tunnel, reachable from the open internet), custom-connector flows for <strong>claude.ai</strong>, <strong>ChatGPT</strong>, and <strong>Gemini</strong>, and the mobile PWA install flow for Notes all work today &mdash; but the edges aren't polished enough to put in front of everyone just yet. We're running these paths through the next couple of weeks of stabilization and will announce each one as it's ready.</p>

        <p>If you want to poke at them early, <code>parachute expose --help</code> is live. Expect rough edges; <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">file issues</a> and we'll fix.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Staying current</h2>

        <p>Once installed, the dispatcher can upgrade itself and every service:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>parachute upgrade</code> &mdash; sweeps hub plus every installed service to the latest npm release.</li>
            <li><code>parachute upgrade hub</code> &mdash; upgrades just the dispatcher.</li>
            <li><code>parachute upgrade vault</code> (or <code>notes</code>, <code>scribe</code>) &mdash; upgrades a single service.</li>
            <li><code>parachute upgrade vault --tag rc</code> &mdash; pin to the <code>rc</code> dist-tag instead of <code>latest</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Vault's schema and filesystem migrations run automatically on first post-upgrade boot &mdash; idempotent, target-wins on conflict, no manual steps required. If you're jumping from the launch 0.2.4 to today, the <a href="/preview/vault-0.4.5-arc/">0.2.4 &rarr; 0.4.5 upgrade arc</a> is the deep dive.</p>

        <p><em><code>parachute upgrade hub</code> requires hub 0.5.9 or later. If you're on an older version, run <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub@latest</code> once to upgrade the dispatcher manually, then <code>parachute upgrade &lt;svc&gt;</code> handles everything else.</em></p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Running it somewhere other than your laptop</h2>

        <p>Your laptop sleeps. If you want your Vault always on, you have options:</p>

        <p><strong>One-click deploy to Render.</strong> The fastest GUI-first "always-on URL" path &mdash; about five minutes from click to a running hub on your own subdomain. Single container, hub-as-supervisor, persistent disk, ~$7/month flat. Modules install onto the disk from the admin UI after first boot. <a href="/deploy/render/">Deploy to Render &rarr;</a></p>

        <p><strong>Or deploy to Fly.io.</strong> The CLI-first peer path &mdash; fork the repo, run <code>./scripts/deploy-to-fly.sh</code>, about the same five minutes, ~$3.34/month all-in. Same architecture, same image; portable volume snapshots if you ever want to move your data. <a href="/deploy/fly/">Deploy to Fly &rarr;</a></p>

        <p><strong>Wait for Parachute Cloud.</strong> We're working on a hosted version — same architecture, same guarantees, no server to run. <a href="https://openparachute.substack.com" target="_blank">Subscribe for updates</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Run it on a Mac or Linux box yourself.</strong> Same flow as your laptop &mdash; <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>, then finish in the wizard (browser or <code>--cli-wizard</code>). On an SSH'd server, <code>init</code> pre-selects Cloudflare exposure so the wizard URL is reachable from your own machine. Any VPS works — Railway, Hetzner, DigitalOcean, EC2, or a home server (Fly and Render get the dedicated walkthroughs above). We don't have step-by-step per-provider guides for the others yet; want one? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">Open an issue</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Inviting friends and family to your hub.</strong> Once your hub is up, you can host it for other people too &mdash; one account per person, each pinned to their own vault, without giving out admin access. <a href="/multi-user/">Multi-user operator guide &rarr;</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Port 1940 already in use.</strong> <code>lsof -i :1940</code> to see what's there. Change ports in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Claude Code doesn't see the MCP.</strong> Start a new Claude Code session (config is loaded at session start). Run <code>/mcp</code> in Claude Code to confirm. If it's missing, <code>parachute-vault mcp-install</code> adds it.</li>
            <li><strong>Want the Vault reachable from another device?</strong> The cleanest path is <code>parachute expose</code> (Tailscale or Cloudflare; polished for broad use in a few weeks, runnable early today via <code>parachute expose --help</code>). If you'd rather expose it yourself &mdash; nginx, Caddy, your own reverse proxy &mdash; set <code>VAULT_BIND=0.0.0.0</code> in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code> and restart so the Vault listens on every interface.</li>
            <li><strong>Stuck on bun 1.2.x?</strong> You may see <code>InvalidPackageResolution: failed to parse lockfile</code> or <code>FileNotFound: failed opening node_modules/package dir</code> &mdash; cosmetic, the package installed anyway. Upgrading to bun 1.3.x makes the warnings go away entirely.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Found something we missed? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">Open an issue</a>.</p>
    </section>

